Function Purpose <code>CURDATE()</code>, <code>NOW()</code> Current date / date-and-time <code>YEAR(d)</code>, <code>MONTH(d)</code>, <code>DAY(d)</code> Extract a part <code>DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL n unit)</code> Add to a date <code>DATEDIFF(d1,d2)</code> Difference in days <code>TIMESTAMPDIFF(unit,d1,d2)</code> Difference in a chosen unit <code>DATE_FORMAT(d, fmt)</code> Format for display <p>Syllabus Q24 — students older than 18:</p> <pre><code>SELECT Name, TIMESTAMPDIFF(YEAR, Dateofbirth, CURDATE()) AS Age
FROM   STUDENT
WHERE  TIMESTAMPDIFF(YEAR, Dateofbirth, CURDATE()) &gt; 18;
</code></pre> <p>Note the expression is repeated: the alias <code>Age</code> cannot be used in <code>WHERE</code>, because <code>WHERE</code> is evaluated before <code>SELECT</code>.</p> <p>Syllabus Q25 (first half) — students born in 2001:</p> <pre><code>SELECT Name FROM STUDENT WHERE YEAR(Dateofbirth) = 2001;
</code></pre> <p>Sargability — why the alternative is faster</p> <p><code>WHERE YEAR(Dateofbirth) = 2001</code> applies a function to the column, so the DBMS must compute it for every row and cannot use an index on <code>Dateofbirth</code>.</p> <p>The range form is equivalent and is index-friendly:</p> <pre><code>WHERE Dateofbirth &gt;= '2001-01-01' AND Dateofbirth &lt; '2002-01-01'
</code></pre> <p>General rule: keep the column bare on one side of the comparison. A condition the optimiser can satisfy with an index is called sargable.</p>
